The section under review is a SQL injection regression, not an authorization flaw. The PR replaces a parameterized `SELECT ... WHERE url IN (?)` query with an f-string that interpolates attacker-influenced URL values (sourced from external hoster plugins via `get_info` → `update_db` → `update_file_info` → `update_link_info`) directly into the SQL statement. This is a CWE-89 (SQL injection) issue, which is outside the scope of the authorization flaw classes I am instructed to report (CWE-639, CWE-862, CWE-863).

There is no authentication/role/ownership check being added, removed, or bypassed in this section, and no object is fetched by a user-controlled identifier without an ownership check in the authorization sense. The policy excerpts retrieved contain no explicit access-control rule that this code violates. Therefore, no authorization candidate flaw is established.

